<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Durotriges</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 44 CE (approx.) · capital Maiden Castle; later Durnovaria (Dorchester)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dorset confederation of hillfort dwellers whose resistance ended when Vespasian's legion stormed strongholds such as Maiden Castle and Hod Hill.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Durotriges took shape in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, a landscape dominated by great multivallate hillforts. Their harbour at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengistbury-head">Hengistbury Head</a> traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a> in the 1st century BCE, bringing in Italian wine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> and Gaulish pottery in exchange for metals, grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power seems to have been spread among several centres rather than held by one king, and no ruler's name appears on their coins, which became crude cast and debased <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> issues. Hillforts such as Maiden Castle were heavily refortified and stocked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a> stones. The Durotriges buried their dead in crouched inhumations, unlike the cremation rites of the south-east, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimmeridge">Kimmeridge</a> shale was worked into bracelets.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 43 and 44 the future emperor Vespasian, leading the legio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-augusta">II Augusta</a>, took more than twenty strongholds in the south-west according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a>; a Roman fort was built inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hod-hill">Hod Hill</a>. The territory became the civitas of the Durotriges with its centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorchester-dorset">Durnovaria</a>, modern Dorchester, within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/durotriges.jpg" alt="Durotriges" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.south.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 44 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maiden Castle; later Durnovaria (Dorchester)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-28&amp;focus=durotriges" aria-label="Durotriges on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4624 418 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Durotriges lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4944 674L5304 942M4944 674L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4944 674h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4944 674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Maiden Castle. <a href="/atlas?year=-28&amp;focus=durotriges">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
